  "summary": "# Health-Tech Daily Digest — October 24, 2026\n\nThe oncology field is generating significant momentum this week, with Moderna and Merck's personalized mRNA melanoma vaccine showing a 42% reduction in recurrence risk at ESMO, while Tango Therapeutics reported an 82% tumor response rate in advanced pancreatic cancer combination therapy. Meanwhile, breakthrough approvals are expanding treatment options: GSK won FDA clearance for Hibsago, a novel hepatitis B drug offering potential functional cure through a new mechanism targeting viral genetic instructions, and GSK's Jemperli gained accelerated approval for advanced rectal cancer with specific genetic signatures. In a related consolidation trend, Merck KGaA is acquiring Silence Therapeutics for $1.1 billion to gain an RNA therapy for polycythemia vera, while Gilead is paying $1.3 billion for an experimental oral peptide targeting inflammatory bowel disease that could rival J&J's newly approved Icotyde.\n\nBeyond oncology, artificial intelligence adoption and oversight are taking center stage as healthcare systems grapple with implementation challenges. The FDA is signaling that AI-enabled clinical tools may need assessment frameworks similar to physician performance, while the AMA is calling for risk-based oversight to clarify accountability as generative AI moves toward more consequential clinical functions. Major employers including Amazon, Google, Meta, and Oracle are backing CMS efforts to expand electronic prior authorization to self-funded plans, underscoring industry frustration with administrative burden. Healthcare IT leaders are also contending with the real-world gap between validated models and clinical outcomes—effective AI requires clear focus on adoption, measurable outcomes, and cost management to deliver genuine value.",
